WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.577
Primitive integers are useful, but they
often say too little.

2
00:00:03.657 --> 00:00:07.266
A `u32` could be a user ID, byte count,
retry count, percentage, timeout,

3
00:00:07.346 --> 00:00:09.417
port, version, score, or protocol field.

4
00:00:09.497 --> 00:00:11.922
The type tells the compiler the
representation,

5
00:00:12.002 --> 00:00:14.487
but it does not tell reviewers the domain
meaning.

6
00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:16.756
Domain types make numeric meaning visible.

7
00:00:16.836 --> 00:00:20.801
A newtype can separate values that share
the same representation but must not be

8
00:00:20.881 --> 00:00:21.236
mixed.

9
00:00:21.316 --> 00:00:24.333
A `UserId` and a `TenantId` might both be
stored as integers,

10
00:00:24.413 --> 00:00:28.081
but a well-designed API should not let one
silently stand in for the other.

11
00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:31.356
Constructors can validate ranges, units,
and invariants once.

12
00:00:31.436 --> 00:00:35.341
A type can represent a bounded percentage,
a positive count, a validated port,

13
00:00:35.421 --> 00:00:38.971
a nonzero size, a safe allocation limit,
or a timeout in a specific unit.

14
00:00:39.051 --> 00:00:42.897
Once constructed, the value carries
evidence that validation has happened.

15
00:00:42.977 --> 00:00:44.989
This reduces scattered numeric checks.

16
00:00:45.069 --> 00:00:47.968
Instead of every caller asking whether a
value is in range,

17
00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:51.834
the program can use a type that is only
constructible when the range is valid.

18
00:00:51.914 --> 00:00:56.056
That makes the safe path easier and makes
invalid states harder to express in normal

19
00:00:56.136 --> 00:00:56.432
code.

20
00:00:56.512 --> 00:00:58.228
Domain types also improve review.

21
00:00:58.308 --> 00:01:01.621
When a function accepts `ByteLimit`
instead of a generic integer,

22
00:01:01.701 --> 00:01:04.068
reviewers know what unit and invariant
matter.

23
00:01:04.148 --> 00:01:07.224
When a parser returns a validated type
instead of a raw number,

24
00:01:07.304 --> 00:01:11.209
downstream code can focus on behavior
rather than revalidating every numeric

25
00:01:11.289 --> 00:01:11.940
assumption.
